  "live_records": {
    "q-10951": {
      "dom_id": "step-q10951",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{n}}{n+2}\\,{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}=\\frac{1}{2}+(x+\\frac{1}{4})+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{4}\\,{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{{3}^{3}}{5}\\,{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}+⋯\\,.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{3}^{n}}{n+2}\\,{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}.$Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{3}^{n+1}{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{n+1}}{n+3}\\cdot \\frac{n+2}{{3}^{n}{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{3(n+2)}{n+3}(x+\\frac{1}{4})| \\\\  & =|x+\\frac{1}{4}|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{3(n+2)}{n+3} \\\\  & =|x+\\frac{1}{4}|\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3|x+\\frac{1}{4}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x+\\frac{1}{4}|<\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{3}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
