    "q-351689": {
      "dom_id": "step-q351689",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x+3)^{n}}{{2}^{{n}^{2}}}=\\frac{x+3}{2}+\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{{2}^{4}}+\\frac{(x+3)^{3}}{{2}^{9}}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x+3)^{n}}{{2}^{{n}^{2}}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\frac{(x+3)^{n+1}}{{2}^{(n+1)^{2}}}}{\\frac{(x+3)^{n}}{{2}^{{n}^{2}}}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x+3)^{n+1}}{{2}^{(n+1)^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{{n}^{2}}}{(x+3)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{x+3}{{2}^{(n+1)^{2}-{n}^{2}}}| \\\\  & =|x+3|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{2n+1}} \\\\  & =|x+3|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$  Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
