    "q-362919": {
      "dom_id": "step-q362919",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-2)^{n}}{n!}=(x-2)+\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{(x-2)^{3}}{3!}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x-2)^{n}}{n!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\frac{(x-2)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}}{\\frac{(x-2)^{n}}{n!}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-2)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\\cdot \\frac{n!}{(x-2)^{n}}| \\\\  & =|x-2|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{n+1} \\\\  & =|x-2|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$  Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
